==Debates==
[[File:Candidatos presidenciales Chile 2009.jpg|thumb|The four candidates attend the second debate organized by Archi.]]

The first debate was organized by [[Televisión Nacional de Chile|TVN]] and took place in Studio #9 at the station's main headquarters in [[Santiago, Chile|Santiago]]. It was broadcast live on 23 September 2009 at 10:40 p.m and included all four candidates. A poll published by Ipsos the following day, showed that Enríquez-Ominami, Arrate and Piñera were each considered to have had the best performance over the rest, with 29-30% of support, while Frei's showing only had the support of 9%. Frei was seen by 45% as the worst performer, followed by Piñera (37%), Arrate (10%) and Enríquez-Ominami (5%).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cooperativa.cl/prontus_nots/site/artic/20090924/asocfile/20090924183914/estudio_primer_debate_presidencial_1.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2009-09-25 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091007013938/http://www.cooperativa.cl/prontus_nots/site/artic/20090924/asocfile/20090924183914/estudio_primer_debate_presidencial_1.pdf |archive-date=2009-10-07 }}</ref> Another poll by ''[[La Segunda]]'' found 23% thought Piñera had won the debate, followed by Arrate (21%), Enríquez-Ominami (15%) and Frei (9%). 31% thought none had won the debate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lasegundadigital.com/modulos/catalogo/Paginas/2009/09/25/LUCSGPO24SG2509.HTM |title=La Segunda |publisher=Lasegundadigital.com |date=2009-09-25 |access-date=2010-02-01 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090928070024/http://www.lasegundadigital.com/modulos/catalogo/Paginas/2009/09/25/LUCSGPO24SG2509.htm |archive-date=28 September 2009 }}</ref>

The second debate was organized by Archi (Radio Broadcasters Association) and [[Mayor University]]. It took place at 8:30 AM on 9 October 2009. It was a radio-only debate, though some local 24-hour news channels broadcast live some parts of it. A poll carried out by Mayor University showed Piñera had won the debate by 41%, followed by Enríquez-Ominami (22%), Arrate (19%) and Frei Ruiz-Tagle (17%).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://latercera.com/contenido/654_190539_9.shtml |title=Encuesta sobre quién ganó el debate: Piñera 41%, Enríquez 22%, Arrate 19% y Frei 17% &#124; PAÍS |publisher=latercera.com |access-date=2010-02-01 |archive-date=13 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110713190204/http://latercera.com/contenido/654_190539_9.shtml |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[File:Candidatos presidenciales 2009 en debate de la ANP.jpg|thumb|left|The four candidates at the ANP debate.]]

There was an online debate on 4 November, organized by [[Terra Networks|Terra]] and [[Radio Cooperativa]]. Only Arrate was present after the other three candidates declined to attend. Frei and Piñera had confirmed their presence in May, while Enríquez-Ominami backed down on the same day of the debate.

A debate to discuss regional issues took place on 6 November at 9 AM in [[Talca]]'s casino. It was organized by the National Press Association (ANP) and was attended by all four candidates.

A fifth debate took place on 9 November at [[Canal 13 (Chile)|Canal 13]]'s studios in [[Santiago, Chile|Santiago]], which was broadcast live at 10 PM. All four candidates were present. This debate was notable because the candidates were able to ask questions to one another and freely talk to each other.

The last debate of the first round was organized by the National Television Association (Anatel) and broadcast live on 16 November at 10 PM by all terrestrial television stations. All candidates attended. There was no audience present.

For the second round, there was a single debate between the two candidates. It was organized by Anatel and broadcast at 10 PM by all terrestrial television stations on 11 January 2010.

==Results==
===President===
On 20 December 2009, the Juntos Podemos Más coalition gave its support to Eduardo Frei's candidacy, after the former president agreed to include a number of policies into his government program.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cooperativa.cl/partido-comunista-oficializo-respaldo-a-frei-en-segunda-vuelta/prontus_nots/2009-12-20/111531.html |title=Partido Comunista oficializó respaldo a Frei en segunda vuelta |publisher=Cooperativa.cl |access-date=2010-02-01}}</ref> Two days later, Jorge Arrate also gave his full support to Frei.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cooperativa.cl/jorge-arrate-sello-respaldo-a-eduardo-frei-en-segunda-vuelta/prontus_nots/2009-12-22/130254.html |title=Jorge Arrate selló respaldo a Eduardo Frei en segunda vuelta |publisher=Cooperativa.cl |access-date=2010-02-01}}</ref> On 13 January 2010 Enríquez-Ominami held a press conference to state that he would vote for Frei, although he did not say his name.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.radiobiobio.cl/2010/01/13/marco-enriquez-ominami-declara-su-apoyo-a-eduardo-frei-pero-deja-en-libertad-de-accion-a-sus-votantes/ |title=Marco Enríquez-Ominami declara su apoyo a Eduardo Frei pero deja en libertad de acción a sus votantes |publisher=Radiobiobio.cl |date=2010-01-13 |access-date=2010-02-01 |archive-date=16 January 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100116022233/http://www.radiobiobio.cl/2010/01/13/marco-enriquez-ominami-declara-su-apoyo-a-eduardo-frei-pero-deja-en-libertad-de-accion-a-sus-votantes/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> He had previously said that voting for Piñera would be a regression and voting for Frei would not be an advancement.
